 when they say system dialog  and start to get ideas and see what you  can do with electron and a cool thing  about this app is that it's an electron  app and the sample code is the actual  code so we can use the node API to fs  read a file and then use the web api to  say the text content of this element is  what we just read from this file and so  in terms of maintaining the app when you  update the app the sample code is also  updated it's not two things you have to  take care of so reoccurring theme is not  giving yourself more work another thing  is that this was a few years  and es6 was still really new and I'm  targeting developed like web developers  who aren't using es6 everywhere and I  didn't want to write just because I  could I didn't want to write a bunch of  JavaScript that looks different to  people and but at the same time wanted  to highlight that a feature of electron  is that you get es6 out of the box and  so the API demos app explains that there  are three bits of es6 that we're using  and here's what they are it was constant  let each string templates that way  people are made aware educating them and  also just not writing a bunch of  different looking code because we can or  it's the new cool thing cuz it's not  about you also this app was built to  open up I wanted people to be able to  split it open look at the insides and  see how an electron app works and so all  of the files and folders are thought  about so that they all relate so that  you can open it up and you can find the  HTML view and the corresponding main and  process main and renderer process files  that go along with it and then there's  documentation about that folder  structure and diagramming of how this  app actually runs from start to  beginning which is something I would  love to see more often I want to know  like what happens when you run this what  happens first okay so I said that I was  prioritizing new developers that didn't  already know what electron was but  doesn't mean I didn't care at all about  the people who are already contributing  electron really needed those people and  needed the electron community and create  a space for this userland  ecosystem to evolve because electron had  been built for atom it had the features  that atom needed it didn't have the  features that a slack or a chat app  needed and so it took it took those team  to contribute those features back to  electron and that was really essential  for making electron better and making it  have tons of features and api's that  atom would never come to on its own  because they were never things that Adam  was going to do and so there is a place  on the site where it's huge now you can  contribute your electron app and the  goal was to help people find each other  so people writing electron apps could  find other people writing electron apps  and hopefully some of them were open  source and people could learn from each  other and then there's userland which  has all the stuff that the users have  built which makes sense for people to do  like building a react specific Wow five  minutes okay  a react specific tool for starting  electron apps with react so then  eventually one more atom person came  over to electron we got head count to  hire one more person there were four of  us we got to God electron to its 1.0 we  moved it all out of the atom org  it got its own org we had our own  meetings and it was really real and then  like Mary Poppins I went on my way for  greener pastures I wanted to work with  more women if I'm real so the electron  team is great now it's a lot different  but anyways that was my own thing so  final thoughts and why I thought any of  this was maybe worth talking about is  that community engineering is something  I when I look back at what I was working  on then and kind of what it would be  called now or what I was doing at all I  think of it as community engineering and  actually now I've seen ipfs do a job  posting for community engineer  ghin shiver at glitch is a community  engineer and and community engineering  is a thing and I think it's great and my  background in the urban design and  architecture I think is not so different  to why I found all of this work really  satisfying because it's about helping or  it's about how people understand and  experience a system and building tools  that put new developers first electrons  situation was also really different and  I think that worked to its advantage it  wasn't like Adam it wasn't Adam was  github vision and an internal vision  that had been shaped and cared about for  a really really long time and so  electron didn't have that electron  wasn't planned it was off kind of to the  side and all of that worked out really  great for electron and electron got to  be in this really unique position where  it was an open source project that  really responded to the community the  community is who had cared about because  there wasn't there wasn't this internal  github vision like there was for Adam  one day though electron will be gone and  it should be because it should be the  stepping stone to the next thing and if  you hate electron and CPU usage you  should hate more if nothing ever  replaces it because that's the problem  but it should be replaced and something  better should come along and I also hope  that our current ideas about  documentation and talking about code  will be gone too another thing I did  outside of like my official electron  capacity was challenging myself to try  to explain electron in the simplest  terms as I could and so I created the  site essential electron with the  limitations of each section has to be  two to three sentences it can't use  jargon if there's industry terms I can't  avoid they're defined below and there's  links on the bottom of each section for  more information and I just want to  - I love that people are doing more  illustrations and diagrams and I just  hope that keeps growing and we change  the way we talk about code and and who  we assume is reading our documentation  and so in general I hope that you see  ways to scale down and actually give  yourself less work so that you can focus  on people and yeah I guess that's it  [Applause]
 

